<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kashf al-Mahjub</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as The Revelation of the Veiled, Kashf al-mahjub</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kashf al-Mahjub, or Revelation of the Veiled, is the oldest surviving treatise on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a> written in Persian, composed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> in the eleventh century by the mystic Ali Hujwiri. It explains Sufi doctrines and practices and gives the lives of earlier saints. Hujwiri's shrine in Lahore, known as Data Darbar, remains one of the most visited in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Al-Hujwiri, also known as Data Ganj Baksh, wrote the book without the library he had left at Ghazna, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, and it must have taken him a long time to compose. Behind it lay at least 40 years of travel in search of knowledge, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, Kohistan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a>, Greater Khorasan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. The book mentions in particular his visits to the shrine of Bilal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and to that of Abu Saeed Abul Khayr in the village of Mihne in Greater Khorasan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The work sets out a complete system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a> with its doctrines and practices, and illustrates the mystical controversies and opinions of its day, many of them clarified from the author's own experience. Hujwiri followed the order of Junayd Baghdadi and so preferred sobriety to intoxication, holding that no one is exempt from the religious law and that Sufism is wholly consistent with the principles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. He wrote that he had met over three hundred saints in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> alone.</p><p>The book defines <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a> and complains that people of the author's age seek pleasure rather than God. It distinguishes ilm, knowledge, from marifat, gnosis: the alim knows only the verbal expression of a thing, while the arif knows its meaning and reality, and the arif depends on his Lord rather than on himself. Of the Sufis he met, Hujwiri names two with the utmost respect, Shaykh Ab al-Abbas Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Ashqani and Shaykh Abu al-Qasim Ali Gurgani.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The book became a medium of spiritual guidance for many later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufis</a>. Moinuddin Chishti, a chief saint of the Chishti order, once stated that a disciple who did not yet have a spiritual master should read it, as it would guide him for the time being. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscripts</a> are preserved in several European libraries, the text was lithographed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, and it has been translated into various languages, into English by Reynold A. Nicholson, who taught Persian and Arabic at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kashf al-Mahjub” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By annexing the Punjab and making Lahore a centre of Persian culture, where the Sufi Ali Hujwiri wrote the <b>Kashf al-mahjub</b>, the Ghaznavids prepared the ground for the Ghurids, the Delhi Sultanate and centuries of Persianate Muslim rule in India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
